  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 348 AM CDT Tue Jul 28 2026\n\nThrough Tonight: Excessive heat and humidity looks to peak today \nover the whole CWA. Early morning satellite imagery/RAP analysis \nshowed the circulation around a large 596dam high centered over the \nsouthern Plains. Our CWA will remain on the eastern periphery of \nthis large high through the forecast period. Early morning surface \nanalysis had high centered over the northern Gulf. This surface high \nwill remain in place through the forecast period. Together high \npressure surface and aloft will contribute to more of the CWA \ntopping out in the upper 90s to near 100F this afternoon. The \ncombination of hot temperatures and high humidity will result in \npeak heat index values above 110F across our south and between the \nMississippi river and Interstate 55. Our western and northeast zones \nwill see peak heat index values between 106F-110F. An \"Extreme Heat \nWarning\" and \"Heat Advisory\" remains in effect today for the respect \nareas. The chance for rain today will be too low to bring any relief \nfrom the heat. /22/\n\nWednesday Onwards...\n\nPortions of south/west MS and northeast LA will still face heat \nconcerns through Wednesday afternoon before clouds/precipitation \nbegin to mitigate heat later this week. As such heat \nadvisories/warnings may need to be extended through Thurs/Fri. A \nfront is expected to sag into the area and stall out through the \narea for the rest of the week. This front should allow for slightly \ndrier and cooler conditions for the northern portions of the CWA \nduring the latter half of the week. Increased chances for convective \nactivity and cloud coverage will also take hold due to the surface \nconfluence from the aforementioned front which could provide a few \ndegree dip; mitigating heat threat concerns. With upper flow \nbecoming more northerly and northwesterly during this time frame, we \nwill see potential for one or two convective complexes moving into \nthe area or developing over the area.\n\nBy the end of the work week, an upper shortwave looks to become \norganized enough that a more typical front will develop across the \nCentral Plains and push into the area around the late \nFriday/Saturday time frame. This will result in continued higher \nrain chances across the area heading into the weekend. It is \nuncertain how much progress this front will make through the area \nbefore stalling, but where it does end up will bring higher than \naverage rain chances across that area to finish out the period./OAJ/",
